1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,315
I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

2
00:00:06,535 --> 00:00:09,730
I was drawing the Sun. Of course I was.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,010
Who draws at six in the morning?

4
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:14,540
MIRA: Artists.

5
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:17,955
And Biscuit was planning a crime.

6
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:21,660
At that moment, half the
world was in daylight.

7
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,340
Biscuit, no!

8
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:31,495
And then the Sun went out.

9
00:00:35,765 --> 00:00:37,445
MIRA: Is it night again?

10
00:00:38,965 --> 00:00:41,530
It can't be night. It's breakfast time.

11
00:00:43,915 --> 00:00:46,705
The lights still worked. The Sun didn't.

12
00:00:47,075 --> 00:00:49,795
Then someone knocked. Three times.

13
00:00:50,675 --> 00:00:55,065
MR. ITO: Good morning, you two. Your mum
just phoned from the hospital.

14
00:00:55,435 --> 00:00:58,405
She's all right. You're with me today.

15
00:00:58,775 --> 00:01:02,320
Put your shoes on, please. Both of you.

16
00:01:02,690 --> 00:01:05,050
Mr. Ito, from upstairs.

17
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:08,880
He was seventy-three, and he
was never in a hurry.

18
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:13,340
His bag was already packed. It
had been packed for years.

19
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:16,955
MR. ITO: And bring the dog.
He has your toast.

20
00:01:17,325 --> 00:01:19,890
It took me a year to understand why.

21
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:23,950
Outside, the whole street was looking up.

22
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:27,290
Mum was a nurse. She couldn't come home.

23
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,430
It's just an eclipse!

24
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:34,435
MR. ITO: An eclipse never lasts more
than seven and a half minutes.

25
00:01:34,805 --> 00:01:38,305
It was happening everywhere,
at the same moment.

26
00:01:38,975 --> 00:01:41,685
In New York, it was two in the morning.

27
00:01:42,355 --> 00:01:46,210
Most of them woke up to a
morning that never came.

28
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,700
MR. ITO: Up to the roof. Kai,
bring my telescope.

29
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:55,280
The lift was full of neighbours, so
Kai carried it up six floors.

30
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:58,010
He complained about every one.

31
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:02,815
Mr. Ito came up here every
morning to watch the sunrise.

32
00:02:03,185 --> 00:02:06,040
That morning, he'd seen the last one.

33
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:09,340
MR. ITO: First, let's find Venus.

34
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:11,845
MIRA: Why is that star still shining?

35
00:02:12,215 --> 00:02:13,955
MR. ITO: That's Venus.

36
00:02:14,325 --> 00:02:20,383
It shines with sunlight bouncing off it, and
that light takes the long way round to

37
00:02:20,383 --> 00:02:21,040
reach us.

38
00:02:21,410 --> 00:02:26,815
If something's happened to the Sun
itself, Venus will go dark too.

39
00:02:27,185 --> 00:02:29,700
At four minutes past seven.

40
00:02:30,070 --> 00:02:31,510
So we waited.

41
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:39,170
MR. ITO: There it goes. So
it's the Sun itself.

42
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:43,005
Jupiter's much further away.

43
00:02:43,375 --> 00:02:46,915
Its light should go out at
four minutes past eight.

44
00:02:47,285 --> 00:02:50,065
At four minutes past eight, it did.

45
00:02:50,835 --> 00:02:53,375
He tapped the telescope three times.

46
00:02:53,745 --> 00:02:56,865
He always did that. I never asked why.

47
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:08,275
(RADIO) Stay indoors and keep warm.

48
00:03:08,645 --> 00:03:11,480
Power and water are working normally.

49
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:14,010
Everyone's going to the shops.

50
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:15,845
We should go too.

51
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:19,975
MR. ITO: I'm ahead of you.
Here's the list.

52
00:03:20,345 --> 00:03:23,890
Kai was right. The whole
city had the same idea.

53
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:26,750
Rice. Tins. Warm socks. Seeds?

54
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:29,965
MIRA: And dog treats.
They're on Biscuit's list.

55
00:03:30,335 --> 00:03:33,845
Two grown men fought over the
last electric heater.

56
00:03:34,215 --> 00:03:37,585
MR. ITO: Heat won't be the
hard part. Food will.

57
00:03:37,955 --> 00:03:40,920
Nothing grows without the Sun.

58
00:03:41,290 --> 00:03:44,145
That evening, at seven, Mum called.

59
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:47,040
Kai! Mira! Oh, there you are.

60
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,555
Mum! Are you okay?

61
00:03:49,925 --> 00:03:52,695
I'm fine, love. I'm so sorry.

62
00:03:53,065 --> 00:03:56,355
I have to stay here. They
need every nurse.

63
00:03:56,725 --> 00:04:00,390
The ambulances haven't
stopped since it went dark.

64
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:02,425
MIRA: When are you coming home?

65
00:04:02,795 --> 00:04:07,465
As soon as I can, sweetheart. I'll
call every night at seven.

66
00:04:07,835 --> 00:04:10,900
Be good for Mr. Ito. I love you.

67
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:14,310
And she did call. Every single night.

68
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:17,395
Mr. Ito slept on our sofa.

69
00:04:17,765 --> 00:04:19,690
Is the Sun dead?

70
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:22,530
MR. ITO: No. I don't think so.

71
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:27,190
If it had vanished, its pull on
the sea would vanish too.

72
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:31,550
And tomorrow's high tide
would be a third lower.

73
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:39,165
Here's a tide table. Go and see for
yourselves. High tide is at twenty to eleven.

74
00:04:39,535 --> 00:04:43,325
So the next morning, Kai and I
walked down to the harbour.

75
00:04:43,695 --> 00:04:45,330
MIRA: It's right on the line!

76
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:47,665
So the Sun's still there.

77
00:04:48,035 --> 00:04:49,795
We just can't see it.

78
00:04:50,165 --> 00:04:51,700
MIRA: Then why is it dark?

79
00:04:52,370 --> 00:04:56,490
The Sun hadn't gone anywhere.
Something was in front of it.

80
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:59,400
MIRA: The fish haven't even noticed!

81
00:04:59,770 --> 00:05:03,360
NEWS ANCHOR: Infrared telescopes have
found something around the Sun.

82
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:05,770
It's warm. It's round.

83
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,905
And it's twice as wide as the Sun.

84
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:12,020
Not a cloud. Not a planet.

85
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:14,910
A shell. Wrapped around a star.

86
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:19,220
NEWS ANCHOR: Governments are
asking everyone to stay calm.

87
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:21,230
Nobody was calm.

88
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,240
MIRA: Mr. Ito? Who built it?

89
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:28,300
MR. ITO: It's bedtime, Mira.

90
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:30,785
It was four in the afternoon.

91
00:05:31,455 --> 00:05:34,070
He'd never sent me to bed before.

92
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:39,530
NEWS ANCHOR: A solar storm that left the Sun
before it went dark is reaching Earth tonight.

93
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:41,855
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

94
00:05:49,025 --> 00:05:51,965
By Day 7, it was twenty below.

95
00:05:52,335 --> 00:05:57,665
The pipes in our building froze and
burst, and the radiators went cold.

96
00:05:58,035 --> 00:06:02,370
All over the world, heating was
never built for cold like this.

97
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:06,225
Mr. Ito showed us how to
keep one room warm.

98
00:06:06,595 --> 00:06:09,735
That night, Kai pretended
he wasn't scared.

99
00:06:10,105 --> 00:06:12,810
He was holding my hand too hard.

100
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:16,805
Then, out on the stairs, we smelled smoke.

101
00:06:17,175 --> 00:06:20,165
MR. ITO: Next door's heating is off too.

102
00:06:20,535 --> 00:06:23,700
They're burning furniture. Indoors!

103
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:25,635
Everyone out!

104
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:29,510
A fire indoors makes carbon monoxide.

105
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:33,685
You can't see it, you can't
smell it, and it kills.

106
00:06:34,055 --> 00:06:36,640
Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

107
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:39,760
At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

108
00:06:40,130 --> 00:06:41,215
MIRA: BISCUIT!

109
00:06:42,585 --> 00:06:44,955
Biscuit! Come on, boy! Please!

110
00:06:45,325 --> 00:06:48,040
The fire brigade put the fire out.

111
00:06:48,410 --> 00:06:52,035
Kai searched all night, and
came back without him.

112
00:06:52,405 --> 00:06:55,285
Mira, sweetheart, listen to me.

113
00:06:55,655 --> 00:06:58,170
Biscuit is a clever dog.

114
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:01,545
Clever dogs find warm places.

115
00:07:02,315 --> 00:07:04,655
MIRA: Kai, where would it still be warm?

116
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:07,465
I don't know. But I'm going to find him.

117
00:07:08,135 --> 00:07:10,095
Kai went straight back out.

118
00:07:10,735 --> 00:07:12,975
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

119
00:07:13,345 --> 00:07:18,700
They led to the only warm thing on the
street: air rising from the metro.

120
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:23,685
You clever, clever dog. You scared me.

121
00:07:24,055 --> 00:07:29,805
A few metres down, the ground stays at
about ten degrees, Sun or no Sun.

122
00:07:30,175 --> 00:07:33,790
Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd
found the way down.

123
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:37,370
By morning, half the street
had followed him down.

124
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:41,420
All over the world, cities
moved underground.

125
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:45,575
By Day 30, Harbour Street
station was a town.

126
00:07:45,945 --> 00:07:50,655
The power stations kept running,
on coal, gas and nuclear.

127
00:07:51,025 --> 00:07:53,960
Electric trains carried food and fuel.

128
00:07:54,330 --> 00:07:59,270
Anyone who could work from home, did.
Everyone else worked underground.

129
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:03,605
We grew lettuce, because it's
ready in a month. Potatoes.

130
00:08:03,955 --> 00:08:07,065
And mushrooms, which don't
need any light at all.

131
00:08:07,435 --> 00:08:10,020
Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

132
00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:14,775
At seven every morning, the
engineers turned on the big lamps.

133
00:08:15,145 --> 00:08:17,525
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

134
00:08:18,645 --> 00:08:22,385
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And
it's warm on your face.

135
00:08:22,755 --> 00:08:26,020
The littlest ones had
started drawing it grey.

136
00:08:26,990 --> 00:08:30,410
I kept my drawing in my pocket. All year.

137
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:34,425
Biscuit got a fan club.
And a bigger belly.

138
00:08:34,795 --> 00:08:38,630
That month, the hospital opened
a clinic in our station.

139
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,840
Mum asked to work there.

140
00:08:41,210 --> 00:08:42,100
MIRA: Mum!

141
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:46,535
And then she was home.

142
00:08:47,705 --> 00:08:50,450
Thank you. For keeping them alive.

143
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:54,025
For looking after them, all this time.

144
00:08:54,395 --> 00:08:57,410
MR. ITO: No. They kept me alive.

145
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:07,145
MIRA: Mr. Ito, you never answered me.

146
00:09:07,515 --> 00:09:08,800
Who built it?

147
00:09:09,515 --> 00:09:12,540
Whoever it was, it must
have taken them ages.

148
00:09:12,910 --> 00:09:16,885
MR. ITO: Much longer than
that. It's very old. Here.

149
00:09:17,555 --> 00:09:22,275
Nineteen-nineteen. A total
eclipse. Look at the lines.

150
00:09:22,645 --> 00:09:24,810
It was always there.

151
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:28,360
Edge-on, like the slats of a blind.

152
00:09:28,730 --> 00:09:33,510
On the morning the Sun went
out, every slat turned.

153
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,320
MIRA: You've seen those lines before.

154
00:09:36,690 --> 00:09:41,385
MR. ITO: Once. A long time
ago. Nobody listened.

155
00:09:42,155 --> 00:09:46,295
I believed him. It was true. It
just wasn't all of it.

156
00:09:47,265 --> 00:09:51,080
On Day 100, it was fifty below up top.

157
00:09:51,450 --> 00:09:55,925
The heaters that warmed the air coming
down into the station froze.

158
00:09:56,295 --> 00:09:57,710
MR. ITO: Let me help.

159
00:09:58,295 --> 00:10:01,660
I know a little about old machines.

160
00:10:02,030 --> 00:10:04,120
That was the coldest night.

161
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:09,595
All over the world, many people didn't
make it through that winter.

162
00:10:10,565 --> 00:10:12,735
Mira? How does this end?

163
00:10:13,905 --> 00:10:15,985
MIRA: With the Sun coming back.

164
00:10:16,955 --> 00:10:22,115
By morning, Mr. Ito and the engineers
had the heaters running again.

165
00:10:22,485 --> 00:10:23,895
MIRA: What are you building?

166
00:10:24,485 --> 00:10:27,755
MR. ITO: Something to listen
with. And to answer.

167
00:10:28,525 --> 00:10:33,410
NEWS ANCHOR: After months of searching, astronomers
have found where the Sun's light is going.

168
00:10:33,780 --> 00:10:39,520
One beam, aimed at a star
called HD 219134.

169
00:10:39,890 --> 00:10:43,955
And there's something inside it,
coming toward us and slowing down.

170
00:10:44,725 --> 00:10:46,140
MR. ITO: They're coming.

171
00:10:47,510 --> 00:10:50,875
NEWS ANCHOR: At this rate, it will
stop in sixty-five days.

172
00:10:51,245 --> 00:10:54,460
Scientists hope the Sun will
come back when it does.

173
00:10:55,230 --> 00:10:57,145
I didn't ask who he meant.

174
00:10:57,815 --> 00:11:02,870
All the Sun's light, pushing against
one small thing, coming our way.

175
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:14,355
On Day 365, the escalators to the
surface had frozen solid.

176
00:11:14,725 --> 00:11:17,785
Kai carried Mr. Ito's dish all the way up.

177
00:11:18,155 --> 00:11:20,120
He didn't complain once.

178
00:11:20,490 --> 00:11:22,930
Half the station came up with us.

179
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:26,940
It was seventy below, so we
wore everything we owned.

180
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:29,320
MIRA: One whole lap around the Sun.

181
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:31,350
Happy birthday, Earth!

182
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:36,700
MR. ITO: It started at 6:52,
exactly one year ago.

183
00:11:37,070 --> 00:11:40,760
I think it stops at 6:52 today.

184
00:11:41,130 --> 00:11:45,195
Listen. Something near the
Sun is calling us.

185
00:11:45,565 --> 00:11:46,375
One.

186
00:11:47,565 --> 00:11:48,125
Two.

187
00:11:49,565 --> 00:11:50,275
Three!

188
00:11:51,565 --> 00:11:53,355
Then it didn't stop.

189
00:11:54,225 --> 00:11:56,665
That's not three. That's hundreds.

190
00:11:57,035 --> 00:12:00,805
MR. ITO: It's stopped. And
listen: they come in rows.

191
00:12:01,175 --> 00:12:04,065
Every row is the same length.

192
00:12:04,735 --> 00:12:06,195
Rows of what?

193
00:12:06,765 --> 00:12:09,530
MR. ITO: Loud beeps and soft beeps.

194
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:13,855
Like squares that are filled
in, or left empty.

195
00:12:14,525 --> 00:12:18,415
We sent a message like
this ourselves, once.

196
00:12:18,785 --> 00:12:21,525
In 1974.

197
00:12:22,195 --> 00:12:26,090
MIRA: Filled squares and empty
squares? That's just graph paper.

198
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:28,025
It's a picture!

199
00:12:28,895 --> 00:12:31,740
MR. ITO: Row by row. Let's see it.

200
00:12:32,895 --> 00:12:36,350
At the top: counting, from one to ten.

201
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:40,890
Then one of them. And one of us,
beside them, much smaller.

202
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:45,455
Their star. And their ship,
parked beside our Sun.

203
00:12:46,425 --> 00:12:48,540
MIRA: Mr. Ito? What is it?

204
00:12:48,910 --> 00:12:50,725
MR. ITO: It's a reply.

205
00:12:51,495 --> 00:12:52,510
MIRA: To who?

206
00:12:53,495 --> 00:12:54,860
MR. ITO: To me.

207
00:13:09,630 --> 00:13:15,455
The young man in the
observatory was Mr. Ito, in 1983.

208
00:13:16,225 --> 00:13:20,995
He found lines in old eclipse
photos. They pointed to one star.

209
00:13:21,365 --> 00:13:23,555
MR. ITO: It was a signpost.

210
00:13:23,925 --> 00:13:28,790
It meant: if you can see
this, call us. So I did.

211
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:32,325
The astronomers he worked
for told him not to.

212
00:13:32,695 --> 00:13:35,740
He knocked anyway. One. Two. Three.

213
00:13:36,510 --> 00:13:41,890
MR. ITO: And they counted on, all the way
to ten, so I'd know it was them.

214
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:45,295
His knock took twenty-one
years to reach them.

215
00:13:45,665 --> 00:13:50,710
They set off the day it arrived. It took
them twenty-three years to get here.

216
00:13:51,080 --> 00:13:54,645
MR. ITO: Something that fast
can't stop by itself.

217
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:02,700
It needs a push from the front. Only a
whole star pushes that hard.

218
00:14:03,070 --> 00:14:09,400
They built that shell long ago, as a
harbour, so one day they could stop here.

219
00:14:09,770 --> 00:14:12,135
How, nobody knows.

220
00:14:12,505 --> 00:14:16,410
So for one year, our Sun was their brake.

221
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:19,760
In a few minutes they'll have stopped.

222
00:14:20,130 --> 00:14:22,870
And they won't need it any more.

223
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:26,820
I knew they might come, any year now.

224
00:14:27,190 --> 00:14:30,105
That's why my bag was packed.

225
00:14:30,875 --> 00:14:32,660
MIRA: Then why didn't they warn us?

226
00:14:33,030 --> 00:14:37,220
MR. ITO: Because a warning can't
travel faster than light.

227
00:14:37,790 --> 00:14:40,355
They were almost that fast.

228
00:14:40,725 --> 00:14:43,315
It would have arrived with them.

229
00:14:44,085 --> 00:14:46,190
MIRA: Did they know it would hurt us?

230
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:48,550
MR. ITO: I don't know.

231
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:52,460
I'm so sorry, Mira.

232
00:14:53,830 --> 00:14:56,050
MIRA: It's okay. You didn't know.

233
00:14:56,420 --> 00:15:00,175
At the bottom of their picture
was an empty square.

234
00:15:00,945 --> 00:15:02,285
MIRA: It's our turn.

235
00:15:03,255 --> 00:15:07,460
Every radio telescope on Earth
got the same picture.

236
00:15:08,030 --> 00:15:11,640
Every government started arguing
about what to say back.

237
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:15,110
MIRA: Can we send this?

238
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:18,420
MR. ITO: Yes. I think we can.

239
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:22,070
My drawing, from the morning it went out.

240
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:27,655
A big yellow sun. And me,
standing under it.

241
00:15:28,525 --> 00:15:30,785
Are we even allowed to do this?

242
00:15:31,155 --> 00:15:32,870
MR. ITO: Probably not.

243
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:38,070
He pressed the key, the
way he had in 1983.

244
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:40,130
Five minutes to go.

245
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:44,540
The whole world was watching
the same patch of sky.

246
00:15:44,910 --> 00:15:46,295
MIRA: What if he's wrong?

247
00:15:46,910 --> 00:15:49,455
Then we'll wait. Together.

248
00:15:59,325 --> 00:16:00,560
MIRA: There it is!

249
00:16:01,730 --> 00:16:06,355
Everywhere it was daytime, the Sun
came back at the same moment.

250
00:16:12,925 --> 00:16:15,735
In New York it was the
middle of the night.

251
00:16:16,105 --> 00:16:20,505
They got their sunrise a few hours
later, the ordinary way.

252
00:16:21,275 --> 00:16:27,250
At 6:52, exactly one year after it went
out, we were standing in sunlight.

253
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:33,900
Every morning for forty-three years,
Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

254
00:16:34,870 --> 00:16:37,980
I used to think it was
because he liked it.

255
00:16:39,150 --> 00:16:41,555
He was waiting for an answer.

256
00:16:43,125 --> 00:16:47,700
Sixteen minutes after we sent my
drawing, the dish clicked again.

257
00:16:50,525 --> 00:16:53,465
What would you have sent?
Tell us in the comments.
